{0 Temporal Phase Semantics}

This note fixes the vocabulary used by the temporal proof generator. It is a
design note, not a new proof strategy.

{1 End-of-instant product states}

A product state is an end-of-instant state:

- the program component is the control state after the program step has run;
- the assumption automaton component is the automaton state after reading the
  instant trace;
- the guarantee automaton component is the automaton state after reading the
  instant trace.

Thus, at instant [n], the product state [(s_n, a_n, g_n)] denotes the state
after instant [n] has completed. The previous product state
[(s_{n-1}, a_{n-1}, g_{n-1})] is the state after instant [n-1] has completed.

{1 Step context}

A program transition from [s_{n-1}] to [s_n] is selected and executed during
instant [n]. Its guard belongs to the step context of instant [n]. It is not a
fact about the end of instant [n-1], and it is not a fact that can be shifted
to instant [n+1] with [pre].

The same distinction applies to assumption-side guards used to select the
current product edge. They constrain the current step. They are not persistent
facts about the next product source state unless a separate semantic rule
proves that persistence.

{1 Meaning of [pre]}

The expression [pre(x)] at instant [n] denotes the value of [x] at the end of
instant [n-1]. More generally, [pre^k(x)] denotes an earlier completed
instant. It never denotes an internal "before executing the previous step"
phase.

Consequently, replacing a step-context fact by [pre] or [pre^2] does not
recover the phase in which a transition guard was evaluated. If the step body
can update a variable, then the value observed by [pre] is the post-step value
of the previous instant, not the pre-body value used by the previous guard.

{1 Current trace point}

The automata for assumptions and guarantees read the current trace point.
Their edges are part of the product step from
[(s_{n-1}, a_{n-1}, g_{n-1})] to [(s_n, a_n, g_n)]. The guarantee edge guard
is therefore a current-step formula: it must be justified from the current
execution and the source product state.

{1 Soundness boundaries}

These boundaries must hold in the generator and in the Rocq development:

- product exploration must not remove an edge because a syntactic conjunction
  mixes source facts and destination facts that look inconsistent;
- the proof generator must not turn a program guard or assumption guard from a
  step-context fact into a previous-tick hypothesis for a later instant;
- reachability of a product state must be represented either by an explicit
  product-reachability argument or by a certified invariant, not by an
  unproved reconstruction from shifted guards;
- for one source precondition, possible guarantee-edge outcomes must remain
  grouped as a disjunction when proving that the program step forces at least
  one safe product successor.

{1 Intended relation}

The intended semantic object is a step relation of the following shape:

{[
reachable_product_state(n - 1, src)
and selected_program_transition(n, tr)
and selected_assumption_edge(n, ae)
and selected_guarantee_edge(n, ge)
and executes_to(n, tr, src.program_state, dst.program_state)
implies reachable_product_state(n, dst)
]}

The important point is that [selected_program_transition],
[selected_assumption_edge], and [selected_guarantee_edge] are facts of instant
[n]. They are not reconstructed at instant [n+1] by applying [pre] to their
guards.

